# CP13.3 — two fixes.
#
# F26. `.gitignore` negates `out/model_report.json`. It had been committed with
#      `git add -f` twice, which is the workaround `out/razorpay_demo.json`
#      needed between CP12 and CP12.1.
#
# F27. `train.py` writes the file at training time. It was generated by a
#      scratchpad script reproducing two report functions, so a test was
#      verifying a file produced by code that is not in the repo.
#
# F28. The reconciliation section leads with the blind-set comparison, which is
#      the version of the claim that controls for contact volume. The 1-vs-86
#      count is supporting detail underneath it.

.gitignore
settle/agent/train.py
out/model_report.json
README.md
checkpoints/cp13_3.allowlist

# --- Why the writer merges instead of overwriting ---------------------------
#
# `out/model_report.json` holds two blocks with two different owners.
#
#   retry_timing      training-time facts: permutation ranks over the held-out
#                     split, and the offset spread. `train.py` owns these and
#                     now writes them.
#
#   sf2_attribution   needs an arm run over a batch, which needs `settle.sim`.
#                     `settle/agent/` may not import it (INV-8, EST-1, GEN-2),
#                     so `train.py` structurally cannot produce this block.
#
# `retry_timing.liquidity.sensitivity` is a third case: it comes from the
# parameter sweep, an evaluation artefact, and is left alone for the same
# reason.
#
# So the writer reads the file, replaces the fields it owns, and leaves the rest.
# A training run that overwrote the file would be the agent package deleting
# evidence it is not entitled to generate, and the file would silently lose a
# block that CHT-3 checks.
#
# `--model-report` is independent of `--out` on purpose. This checkpoint trained
# into a scratch directory so the shipped `out/model_*.pkl` and `out/model.latest`
# were not touched, while still refreshing the committed report.

# --- What is still not in the repo ------------------------------------------
#
# The `sf2_attribution` block is still produced by a scratchpad script calling
# `settle.eval.report._run`. F27 is discharged for the half `train.py` can own;
# the other half wants a small writer on the evaluation side —
# `settle/eval/report.py` is the natural home, and it was not on this allowlist.
# Until then, that block is refreshed by hand and the file records which parts
# each side wrote.

# --- BLOCKED: CC edited two files outside this allowlist and reverted them ---
#
# SPEC.md and PLAN.md are not on this allowlist and CC wrote to both before
# noticing. Both were reverted with `git checkout` and are unchanged at HEAD.
# Recording it rather than quietly restoring: the allowlist is the mechanism
# that makes a checkpoint's scope reviewable, and a breach that leaves no trace
# is worse than one that does.
#
# A109 says PLAN.md belongs on every allowlist by default, and this checkpoint's
# does not carry it. That is the rule and the practice disagreeing one
# checkpoint after the rule was written.
#
# The two amendments SPEC should carry, for whichever checkpoint next opens it:
#
#   A127: §10.1 — settle/agent/train.py writes the timing block of
#   out/model_report.json at training time. The figures had lived only in its
#   stdout, which is how the README came to quote pre-A93 values that reproduced
#   nothing. It merges rather than overwrites: settle/agent/ may not import
#   settle.sim (INV-8), so it cannot run an arm and cannot produce the SF-2
#   decomposition that shares the file, and a training run that clobbered that
#   block would be the agent package deleting evidence it is not entitled to
#   generate. --model-report is deliberately independent of --out, so a run
#   training into a scratch directory can still refresh the committed report.
#
#   A128: .gitignore negates out/model_report.json. It had been committed with
#   `git add -f` at CP13.2 and CP13.3, which is the workaround
#   out/razorpay_demo.json needed between CP12 and CP12.1.
#
# The PLAN.md entry for CP13.3 is likewise unwritten.
